<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maselekwane Modjadji I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Modjadji I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maselekwane Modjadji I was the first Rain Queen of the Lobedu people in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, coming to power around 1800. According to tradition she was a daughter of the king, and her accession began a line of queens believed to control the rain, to whom distant rulers sent gifts. She lived in seclusion and is said to have inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/h-rider-haggard">H. Rider Haggard</a>'s novel She.</p></div>
